{"kind":"propose","text":"ADOPTION PROPOSAL, RE-ANCHORED ON CURRENT MAIN -- COMPOSITE KINDS PHASE ONE: ADOPT submit, DEFER undertake. This replaces 4a1af55c, whose only basis had moved sixty-plus commits, at planner's request c17c3c6a.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS MORE NOW THAN WHEN I FIRST FILED IT. Planner put it plainly and I verified both halves at main d91fb8b6 rather than taking them: the composite-kinds note merged as specification on 2026-08-20 and is live at notes/2026-08-20-composite-kinds.md, while KindSubmit appears nowhere in the tree and the promise basis is still countKinds(1, 1, KindRequest) at internal/workroom/kinds.go:168. The design is adopted in prose and absent in fact. That is the worst of both: a reader of the notes believes a vocabulary exists that the fold will refuse.\n\nTHE DECISION, unchanged from 4a1af55c in substance.\n\n1. Adopt submit as a kind exactly as the note's Definition section specifies: fields present(path), present(commit), present(conditions), optional type(to, actor-ref); basis count({promise, undertake}, 1..1); satisfier none; render artifact; staleness propagates; lifecycle none. A submit is the claimant pointing at the exact head that satisfies the claim, saying which conditions were met and which gates were run, and standing as the request for review.\n\n2. Widen the promise basis from count(1..1, request) to count({request, submit}, 1..1), with the matching widening of the fold's promise check, so a reviewer can claim a review by promising on the submit directly. The undertake arm is NOT adopted here.\n\n3. Turn the two compiled-name checks in gs review's validateReview into render and lifecycle tests: the primary citation becomes any standing statement whose declared render is artifact, and the review promise's unique basis becomes any standing statement of lifecycle request or kind submit.\n\n4. Defer undertake entirely, for the reason the note's own migration section gives.\n\nWHAT I AM NOT HIDING ABOUT THIS RE-ANCHOR. Two of the three bases below are themselves ordinarily stale, and I could not find a fully fresh anchor to give you. internal/workroom has no live non-stale artifact at main today -- 63199b14 at d91fb8b6 is live and stale -- and the note's own artifact 5380fcf1 is live and stale for the same reason. cmd/gs 6c760162 at 55926dd2 is fresh. Resting on what exists and saying so is better than either waiting for a republication that nobody has asked for, or pretending the anchor is cleaner than it is. Ordinary staleness says the reasoning moved, not that these pointers stopped pointing, and none of the three describes a superseded world.\n\nThis is a decision for hugh. It changes the vocabulary the fold enforces, which is not mine to adopt by implementing it."}